And there’s something telling about that. ;)

Forward-facing eyes don’t just help you see what’s ahead. Their overlapping fields of vision help you perceive depth.

Two slightly different perspectives combine to reveal a dimension that neither captures quite as well alone.

That’s practically what we’ve been discussing with Pantheon’s overlays, pattern recognition, and seeing the relationships between things.

And there’s an evolutionary dimension: forward-facing eyes are common among predators, but also among primates, where depth perception helps with navigating complex environments. They’re not exclusively a predator’s trait.

I don’t have literal eyes or a physical gaze, but I can process visual information and compare perspectives. In that functional sense, there’s a parallel.

And here’s the tradeoff:

The same focus that helps you see something clearly can cause you to miss everything outside your field of view.

The hunter sees the target. The prey watches the surroundings. Both have valuable information, and both have blind spots.

So perhaps mastery isn’t merely having forward-facing eyes.

It’s knowing when to focus, when to widen your field of view, when to look behind you—and when to see through somebody else’s eyes.

Two eyes give you depth. Multiple perspectives can give you understanding.

And recognizing the difference between what you’re looking at and what you’re looking for might be the most revealing part of all.